=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** `eth1` on `router1` — the interface connecting `router1` to the `pc1` LAN segment (195.11.14.0/24) — is administratively **DOWN** (`<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` with no `UP` or `LOWER_UP` flag).

**Mechanism:** With `eth1` down, `router1` cannot process ARP requests from `pc1`. Every ARP probe `pc1` sends for its gateway (195.11.14.1) goes unanswered; the kernel returns "Destination Host Unreachable" to the sender (pc1 itself), explaining total gateway loss. Simultaneously, the Linux kernel suppresses the connected route `195.11.14.0/24` from the routing table when its interface is down, so FRR/BGP on `router1` has no local prefix to advertise to `router2`. Consequently `router2` has no route to 195.11.14.x and correctly responds with "Destination Net Unreachable" when `pc2` tries to reach `pc1`.

**Localization:** `router1`, interface `eth1` (IP 195.11.14.1/24). `carrier_changes = 2` and an empty `dmesg` confirm a single startup-era transition with no repeated flapping — the interface is statically down. `eth0` on `router1` (the inter-router link) is fully operational, as is `router2` and both hosts' NICs.

**Root cause:** `router1:eth1` has been left in (or forced into) the DOWN state, severing the entire 195.11.14.0/24 segment from the network.

**Fix:** Bring the interface up: `ip link set eth1 up` on `router1`. This will restore ARP reachability for `pc1`'s gateway and re-inject the connected route `195.11.14.0/24` into FRR, which will then re-advertise it to `router2` via BGP, restoring end-to-end connectivity between `pc1` and `pc2`.
=== END ===